<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-lhopitals-rule">
  <title>L'Hôpital's Rule</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-lhopitals-rule">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Use the definition of the derivative and the sum rule for exponents to write <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\big[b^x\big]</m> as <m>b^x</m> times a limit, and identify that limit as <m>\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize limits of the indeterminate forms <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> and <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, and explain why substitution alone cannot decide their values.</p></li>
      <li><p>Verify the requirements of L'Hôpital's rule before using it, and use it to replace a limit with a new limit formed from the ratio of derivatives.</p></li>
      <li><p>Apply L'Hôpital's rule repeatedly when the new limit is again an indeterminate form.</p></li>
      <li><p>Rewrite products of the form <m>0\cdot\infty</m> as ratios, and rewrite limits with the variable in both base and exponent using base <m>e</m>, so that L'Hôpital's rule can be used.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We previously found the derivative of an exponential function by differentiating a
      logarithm. Today we will connect that result to the definition of the derivative,
      identifying a limit that none of our earlier techniques can
      evaluate. This will motivate a new tool for computing limits called L'Hôpital's rule.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-exponential-derivative-definition" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Derivative of an Exponential, Revisited</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Recall that the derivative of a function <m>f</m> is defined by
        <md>
          <mrow> f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} </mrow>
        </md>.
        Let <m>b\gt 0</m> and let <m>\exp_b(x)=b^x</m>. We will apply this definition to <m>\exp_b</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-difference-quotient" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the difference quotient for <m>\exp_b</m> and use the sum rule for exponents,
          <m>b^{x+h}=b^x\cdot b^h</m>, to factor <m>b^x</m> out of the numerator. Explain why
          <m>b^x</m> can then be moved outside the limit as <m>h \to 0</m>. Show that
          <md>
            <mrow> \exp_b'(x) = b^x \cdot \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{b^h-1}{h} </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-try-to-evaluate" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try to evaluate <m>\displaystyle\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{b^h-1}{h}</m> by substituting <m>h=0</m>.
          What do the numerator and denominator each do as <m>h \to 0</m>? Can you tell what the
          limit is from this information alone?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-no-cancel" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In earlier work, a limit of a quotient of simple algebraic formulas that gave 
          an indeterminate form of 0/0 could be rewritten by canceling a common factor.
          Is there a common factor of <m>b^h-1</m> and <m>h</m> that you can cancel? 
          Why is this limit different?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-solve-for-limit" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Last time, we found a different formula for the same derivative: using <m>y=b^x</m>,
          we showed that <m>\exp_b'(x)=b^x\ln(b)</m>. Comparing the two formulas,
          identify the limit <m>\displaystyle\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{b^h-1}{h}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-limit-equals-ln-b">
    <title>A Limit We Could Only Find Indirectly</title>
    <p>
      For every <m>b\gt 0</m>,
      <md>
        <mrow> \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{b^h-1}{h} = \ln(b) </mrow>
      </md>.
      We found this value recognizing the limit matches a derivative,
      not by evaluating the limit itself.
      A limit whose numerator and denominator both approach <m>0</m> is said to have the
      <term>indeterminate form</term> <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>. It is called indeterminate because the
      form alone does not decide the answer: such a limit might equal any number, or might not
      exist at all. We need to find a <em>different</em> formula for the limit, one that
      <em>can</em> be evaluated.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-cancel-common-factor" workspace="0in">
    <title>Rewriting a Limit by Canceling a Common Factor</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the limit <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{x^2-4}{x^2-3x+2}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-cancel-substitute" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to the numerator and the denominator as <m>x \to 2</m>? Why can we not
          simply evaluate the limit by substitution?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-cancel-factor" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the numerator and the denominator. Identify the common factor and use it to
          <em>rewrite</em> the quotient as a simpler formula that agrees with the original for
          every <m>x\ne 2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-cancel-evaluate" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why the limit of the original quotient as <m>x \to 2</m> is the same as the limit
          of your simplified formula. Why can the simplified limit be found by evaluation? What is
          the value of the limit?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-lhopitals-rule">
    <title>L'Hôpital's Rule</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>f(x) \to 0</m> and <m>g(x) \to 0</m> as <m>x \to a</m>, and
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)} = L</m>, then
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = L</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-how-to-read-the-rule">
    <title>What the Rule Says and How to Use It</title>
    <p>
      Canceling a common factor <em>rewrites</em> a limit of the form <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> as a new
      limit that we can evaluate. L'Hôpital's rule does the same job for limits where there is no
      common factor to cancel: it lets us <em>rewrite</em> the limit using a new formula that we
      hope can be evaluated.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice that we are <em>not</em> computing the derivative of the quotient
      <m>\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}</m>, and we do not use the quotient rule. We are creating a new
      <em>limit</em> whose formula is the ratio of the two derivatives <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g'(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rule has two requirements. <em>Requirement 1:</em> <m>f(x) \to 0</m> and
      <m>g(x) \to 0</m> as <m>x \to a</m>. <em>Requirement 2:</em> the limit
      <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}</m> has a value <m>L</m>. We use
      the rule only <em>after</em> checking these requirements. Once they are satisfied, the rule
      is the <em>justification</em> for replacing the original limit with the simpler one.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-revisit-rational-example" workspace="0in">
    <title>Checking the Requirements on a Familiar Limit</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{x^2-4}{x^2-3x+2}</m>. Here
        <m>a=2</m>, <m>f(x)=x^2-4</m>, and <m>g(x)=x^2-3x+2</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-revisit-requirement-1" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>f(2)=0</m> and <m>g(2)=0</m>. Since <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are continuous,
          explain why this means <m>f(x) \to 0</m> and <m>g(x) \to 0</m> as <m>x \to 2</m>, so
          Requirement 1 is satisfied.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-revisit-requirement-2" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g'(x)</m>. Form the new limit
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}</m> and evaluate it. Does this
          limit have a value, so that Requirement 2 is satisfied?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-revisit-conclusion" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State the conclusion that L'Hôpital's rule gives for
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{x^2-4}{x^2-3x+2}</m>. Does it agree with the
          value you found by canceling the common factor?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-arrow-notation">
    <title>A Shorthand for Requirement 1</title>
    <p>
      Checking Requirement 1 comes up so often that it helps to have a shorthand that does not
      require naming the functions. We write
      <md>
        <mrow> \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \rightarrow \frac{0}{0} </mrow>
      </md>
      to mean that <m>f(x) \to 0</m> and <m>g(x) \to 0</m> as <m>x \to a</m>. The arrow reports
      what the numerator and denominator are doing. It does not say that the limit equals
      <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-exponential-over-polynomial" workspace="0in">
    <title>A Limit with No Common Factor</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{3^x-9}{x^2-4}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-exp-poly-evaluate" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why can we not simply evaluate this limit? Can you cancel a common factor?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-exp-poly-requirement-1" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify the numerator function <m>f(x)</m> and the denominator function <m>g(x)</m>.
          Does this limit satisfy Requirement 1? Record your conclusion using the shorthand
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{3^x-9}{x^2-4} \rightarrow \frac{0}{0}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-exp-poly-requirement-2" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now that Requirement 1 is verified, find <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g'(x)</m>, and write the
          new limit <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}</m>. Can this limit be
          evaluated?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-exp-poly-answer" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the value of <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 2} \frac{3^x-9}{x^2-4}</m>, and which
          statement justifies your answer?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-repeated-application" workspace="0in">
    <title>Using the Rule More Than Once</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Some limits require repeating the process. Consider
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{e^{2x}-1-2x}{x^2}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-repeat-first-check" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the arrow notation to decide whether this limit satisfies Requirement 1.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-repeat-first-derivative" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <em>After</em> this verification, create the new limit formed from the ratio of the
          first derivatives of the numerator and denominator. Try to evaluate it. What do you find?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-repeat-second-derivative" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Verify Requirement 1 for this new limit using the arrow notation. <em>After</em> this
          second verification, create another new limit using the ratio of the second derivatives.
          Can this limit be evaluated?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-repeat-work-backwards" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now work backwards. Which of the three limits did you learn the value of first? Explain
          how L'Hôpital's rule then gives the value of the middle limit, and then the value of the
          original limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-infinity-over-infinity">
    <title>The Form <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m></title>
    <p>
      L'Hôpital's rule <em>also</em> works when the numerator and denominator both grow without
      bound. If <m>f(x) \to \pm\infty</m> and <m>g(x) \to \pm\infty</m> as <m>x \to a</m> (or as
      <m>x \to \pm\infty</m>), and <m>\displaystyle\lim \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}=L</m>, then
      <m>\displaystyle\lim \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=L</m>. Requirement 1 is simply replaced by checking
      that the limit has the form <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m> in the arrow notation.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-infinity-over-infinity" workspace="0in">
    <title>Limits at Infinity</title>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-infinity-evaluate" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{100x}{e^{0.01x}}</m>. Begin by verifying
          the form with the arrow notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-products">
    <title>Products: The Form <m>0\cdot\infty</m></title>
    <p>
      A limit of a product <m>f(x)\,g(x)</m> where <m>f(x) \to 0</m> and
      <m>g(x) \to \pm\infty</m> is also indeterminate. L'Hôpital's rule only applies to ratios,
      so the first step is to <em>rewrite</em> the product as a ratio. Moving one factor to the
      denominator as its reciprocal produces a limit of the form <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> or
      <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, and then we can check the requirements.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-product-form" workspace="0in">
    <title>Rewriting a Product as a Ratio</title>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-product-form-identify" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 0^+</m>, what does each factor of <m>x\ln(x)</m> do? Why is this limit not
          yet in a form where L'Hôpital's rule applies?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-product-form-evaluate" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite <m>x\ln(x)</m> as a ratio by writing <m>x=\dfrac{1}{1/x}</m>, verify the form with
          the arrow notation, and find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0^+} x\ln(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-lh-powers">
    <title>Powers: Variable in the Base and the Exponent</title>
    <p>
      A limit of the form <m>\displaystyle\lim f(x)^{g(x)}</m>, where the variable appears in both
      the base and the exponent, cannot be attacked directly. Instead, rewrite the expression using
      base <m>e</m> and logarithms:
      <md>
        <mrow> f(x)^{g(x)} = e^{g(x)\ln(f(x))} </mrow>
      </md>.
      Then find the limit of the <em>exponent</em> <m>g(x)\ln(f(x))</m>. Because <m>e^x</m> is
      continuous, the limit of interest is <m>e</m> raised to the limit you found for the exponent.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-lh-variable-in-exponent" workspace="0in">
    <title>Variable in the Base and the Exponent</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0} (1+3x)^{2/x}</m>.
        It is not in the form of a ratio where L'Hôpital's rule would be relevant,
        so we will transform the formula before testing if it is indeterminate.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-power-limit-form" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          As <m>x \to 0</m>, what limits do the base <m>1+3x</m> and the exponent <m>\frac{2}{x}</m>
          each appraoch? Use arrow notation to indicate this limit form.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-power-rewrite" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite <m>(1+3x)^{2/x}</m> using base <m>e</m> and <m>\ln</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-power-exponent-limit" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the limit of the exponent
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2\ln(1+3x)}{x}</m>, verifying the form first.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-lh-power-conclusion" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the continuity of <m>e^x</m> to find
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} (1+3x)^{2/x}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
